      ":question/worked-solution": "Since our ellipse is centered at the origin and has semiaxes $3$ and $4,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1\\,\u27f9\\,\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{y}^{2}}{16}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{9}+\\frac{2y}{16}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2x}{9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{16x}{9y}\\end{aligned}\n$$"
    },
